  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Given that $5-\\text{i}=(p+\\text{i}q)(3+2\\text{i})$, calculate $p$ and $q.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we divide both sides of the equation by $3+2\\text{i}$ to isolate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5-\\text{i} & =(p+\\text{i}q)(3+2\\text{i}) \\\\ \\frac{5-\\text{i}}{3+2\\text{i}} & =p+\\text{i}q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to simplify the fraction on the left-hand side, we multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(5-\\text{i})(3-2\\text{i})}{(3+2\\text{i})(3-2\\text{i})} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{15-3\\text{i}-10\\text{i}+2{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{3}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2}} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{13-13\\text{i}}{9+4} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{13-13\\text{i}}{13} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ 1-\\text{i} & =p+\\text{i}q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get $1=p.$\n\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get $-1=q.$\n\nTherefore, $p=1$ and $q=-1.$",
